A scalable web infrastructure is designed to improve performance, availability, and maintainability by separating components and adding redundancy.

The infrastructure includes:
- 2 load balancers (HAProxy) configured as a cluster
- Multiple servers with separated roles:
  - Web server (Nginx)
  - Application server
  - Database server (MySQL)

Why adding an additional load balancer:
Adding a second load balancer removes the single point of failure. If one load balancer fails, the other can continue handling traffic.

Load balancer cluster:
The two load balancers are configured in a cluster (Active-Passive or Active-Active) to ensure high availability.

Why separating components:
Each component (web server, application server, database) runs on its own server:
- Web server handles HTTP requests and static content
- Application server processes business logic
- Database server manages data storage

Benefits of separation:
- Better scalability (each layer can be scaled independently)
- Improved performance
- Fault isolation (failure in one component does not affect others directly)

Application server vs web server:
The web server (Nginx) handles incoming requests and serves static files.
The application server runs the application logic and generates dynamic responses.

Database:
The database server is dedicated to storing and managing data, which improves performance and reliability.
